
## problem is to find the next element that is smaller or equal to curr

stack -> find next element, we want to compare backwards
monotonic increasing stack -> if the element is >= curr, we can apply current as discount

example - 
[8, 4, 6, 2, 3]

notice: 
2 is discount for 4 and 6, if we keep a monotonic increasing stack to record the previous items,
if we see the in stack element is greater than curr, the curr is discount to that element, also pop the element (because discount already applied)


```c++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> finalPrices(vector<int>& prices) {
        stack<int> s;
        vector<int> res (prices.begin(), prices.end());
        for(int i = 0; i < prices.size(); ++i){
            int curr = prices[i];
            while(!s.empty() && curr <= prices[s.top()]) {
                res[s.top()] -= curr;
                s.pop();
            }
            s.push(i);
        }

        return res;
    }
};
```

────────────────────────────────────────
Reverse approach (monotonic stack, right to left)
────────────────────────────────────────
Key: pop while stack.top() > curr (strictly greater!)
     Do NOT pop equal values — equal is a valid discount.
     After popping, stack.top() is the next ≤ element (the discount).

```c++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> finalPrices(vector<int>& prices) {
        int n = prices.size();
        vector<int> res(prices.begin(), prices.end());
        stack<int> s; // stores values (not indices)

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            // pop elements strictly greater than current
            while (!s.empty() && s.top() > prices[i])
                s.pop();
            // if stack not empty, top is the next <= element
            if (!s.empty())
                res[i] -= s.top();
            s.push(prices[i]);
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

⚠️  Pitfall: if you pop `>=` instead of `>`, you discard equal
     values that are valid discounts.
     e.g. [2,2,1] → wrong: [1,1,1], correct: [0,1,1]

